infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title The five numbers that sit on the "one box vs a fleet" line
  desc Each estimate straddles a threshold where the architecture bends — compute these, skip the rest
  lists
    - label Peak read QPS
      desc One primary serves low-thousands of reads; cross it and you need replicas, a cache, or a fan-out read path. Usually the first design-changing number.
    - label Peak write QPS
      desc One primary handles low-thousands of writes; tens of thousands forces sharding or a write-optimized store. Decides whether the data tier is simple or hard.
    - label Storage by data class
      desc Text vs media often differ ~100x; the dominant class picks the technology — relational/wide-column for text, object store + CDN for media.
    - label Hot working-set size
      desc Fits one node's RAM (~256 GB) and a single cache node works; above it, you shard the cache across nodes.
    - label Peak egress bandwidth
      desc Peak read QPS x avg response size sets the CDN bill and the NIC limit — e.g. ~12 Gbps won't fit a single 10 Gbps NIC.
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6 #f59e0b #f43f5e
